        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "617199c7-74b0-510f-a0bd-2eed7c62e96f",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: Mount Ida, Crete, ancient Troad, western Anatolia, Turkey, Amari Valley, Mount Kedros\n- People: Rhea, Gaia, Uranus, Cronus, Homer, Virgil\n- Concepts: Greek mythology, classical antiquity\n- Works: Iliad, Aeneid\n\nFacts:\n- In Greek mythology, the name Mount Ida refers to two sacred mountains called the \"Mountain of the Goddess.\"\n- One Mount Ida is located in Crete.\n- The other Mount Ida is in the ancient Troad region of western Anatolia in modern-day Turkey.\n- In classical antiquity, the Mount Ida in the Troad was also known as the \"Phrygian Ida.\"\n- Homer mentions the Mount Ida in the Troad in the Iliad.\n- Virgil mentions the Mount Ida in the Troad in the Aeneid.\n- Rhea is a Titaness in Greek mythology.\n- Rhea is the daughter of Gaia and Uranus in Greek mythology.\n- Rhea is the sister and wife of Cronus in Greek mythology.\n- The Amari Valley is a fertile valley in Crete.\n- The Amari Valley lies on the foothills of Mount Ida and Mount Kedros in Crete.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "c3c6a8d9-ce47-590f-abdc-cfcd26e7f7a6",
        "text": "Page: Mount Ida. Sentence: In Greek mythology, two sacred mountains are called Mount Ida, the \"Mountain of the Goddess\": Mount Ida in Crete; and Mount Ida in the ancient Troad region of western Anatolia (in modern-day Turkey) which was also known as the \"Phrygian Ida\" in classical antiquity and is the mountain that is mentioned in the Iliad of Homer and the Aeneid of Virgil.\nPage: Rhea (mythology). Sentence: Rhea ( ; Greek: Ῥέα , ] ) is a character in Greek mythology, the Titaness daughter of the earth goddess Gaia and the sky god Uranus, and sister and wife to Cronus.\nPage: Amari Valley. Sentence: The Amari Valley is a fertile valley on the foothills of Mount Ida and Mount Kedros in Crete.",
